#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""What a session cost in Claude usage, from Claude Code's local transcripts (KAN-613).

    scripts/dev/usage-report                  the last ten ticket sessions of this repository
    scripts/dev/usage-report --last 20        the last twenty
    scripts/dev/usage-report --all            every session, ticket or not
    scripts/dev/usage-report --session 2c20e3ae --session d81bd30b
    scripts/dev/usage-report --since 2026-11-01 --exclude b0b03e77
    scripts/dev/usage-report --selftest       checks this script against a built transcript

Where the transcripts are: Claude Code writes one JSON-lines file per session to
`~/.claude/projects/<folder>/<session id>.jsonl` (`$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/projects` when that
is set). The folder is the session's working directory with every character that is not a
letter or digit replaced by `-`, so this repository's main checkout and each worktree under
`.claude/worktrees/` have folders of their own. A sub-agent's transcript sits in
`<folder>/<session id>/subagents/` (workflows one level further down), next to a
`.meta.json` naming its agent type. A session that moved between worktrees has parts in
several folders; they are read as one session. Cloud sessions run on Anthropic's machines:
their transcripts are not on this Mac and are not reported.

What is counted, per agent (the main session and each sub-agent): API calls, then input,
cache-read, cache-write and output tokens as the transcript recorded the API's usage. A
response is written as several records that share one message id; each call is counted once.
A call none of whose records carries a stop reason has only a streaming snapshot of its
usage (older transcripts, sub-agents especially): its input side is complete, its output is
a lower bound, and the report says how many such calls each agent has.

Where the input went: every call re-reads the whole context, so what a call reads is the
previous call's context plus whatever entered since - the model's previous output and the
tool results, attachments and prompts recorded in between. The growth between two calls is
measured (the API's token counts), its first part is the model's own output, and the rest
is split among what entered in that step in proportion to its size. An item then costs its
tokens once for every later call that still carries it, until a compaction. These shares
add up exactly to the input-side total. No item is given more than one token per 1.4
characters (an image: 2,000 tokens) - denser than 99 in 100 measured results - and, in a
step where a ToolSearch ran, no more than the agent's measured rate; growth beyond that is
the schemas the ToolSearch loaded, when there was one, else "growth with no recorded source"
(system reminders the harness does not log). Only the part before a segment's first call
(system prompt, tool schemas, CLAUDE.md, the opening prompt) is not measured step by step:
there CLAUDE.md and the prompt are estimated at the agent's own measured characters per
token (the median of its steps that added one item of 500+ characters, a reminder beside it
aside; 2.25 when it has fewer than five - the median over every local session measured
2.25 to 2.28 on 2026-11-10, depending on which steps count) and the rest is "session start".

A sub-agent's type comes from its .meta.json, else from the main session's Agent call
that started it (the call's result names the agent id the transcript file carries).

The summary's columns, each a share of the session's total tokens (every agent, input side
and output), which overlap by design:
  Rules       .claude/rules files and CLAUDE.md/MEMORY.md, carried, in every agent
  Large reads single file reads (Read, or cat/sed/head in Bash) that added >= 5,000 tokens
  Reviewer    everything the reviewer sub-agents spent
  Notion      Notion tool results, carried, in every agent
  CI polling  the calls spent reacting to a CI poll (gh pr checks, check-runs, gh run, the
              PR-status tool): each re-reads the whole context

This prints counts, tool names, agent types, ticket ids, session ids, branch names, Notion
page ids and file paths (repository-relative; a file outside the repository by its base
name). A Bash command is read only through its skeleton - heredoc bodies, comments and
quoted text with a space in it removed - and labelled by a program from a fixed list
(KNOWN_PROGRAMS, else "other") or, for a view (cat, head, tail, nl, less, sed -n), by the
file it reads - and that only when the word names a file that exists on this machine now,
the label made from the path the operating system resolved to that file (else "?"), so no
misread option value, sed script or heredoc can be printed. A file read
with Read is named by the path the call was given. It never prints what a transcript says -
no prompt, result, command or answer text - and it opens no network connection (it reads
the filesystem only: the transcripts, and whether a viewed file exists). Standard library
only.
"""

